# Every classification this tree makes, declared. One row per classifying relation: the relation,
# the type its classes belong to, and what one counted row of it IS.
#
# `subject` is the unit of the count, and it is declared because it cannot be recovered
#   A census counts rows, and a row of `arithmetic/all.sqlc` is not the same kind of thing as a
#   row of `checks/fit_axes.sqlc`. Eight relations here count in six different units, so a number
#   read across them means nothing and a threshold set across them would be wrong six ways. The
#   column is `checks/roster.sqlc`'s, the same word for the same job: `examples/soundness/main.rs`
#   §4 already holds it for rules, and *"the coverage number has a UNIT now"* is what that bought.
#
# The relation is not derived from the tree
#   A contract must not be derived from what it governs, so this is a `VALUES` literal like every
#   other roster here. Read the eight names off `epistemics/class_cells.sqlc` and this file could
#   never disagree with it, and `algebra/class_domain.sqlc` would be anti-joining a set against
#   itself.
#
# `filed` says whether a ball is something a document filed
#   Six of the eight classify rows somebody filed, so restricting them to a set of documents is a
#   real question and reports/class_census.sqlc asks it. Three do not: this tree's own rules are
#   not filed by anybody. Their classes are identical under every scope, so a scoped report must
#   leave them out rather
#   than print a zero that reads like a finding. algebra/class_domain.sqlc holds the flag against
#   whether any ball of that relation carries a filing, so the two cannot drift.
#
# `codomain` is the type's bare name, never a rendered one
#   `pg_typeof(x)::text` spells a type through the session's `search_path`, so the same class
#   prints `pm.fit` to one client and `fit` to another, and a literal here would be right in one
#   session and wrong in the next. epistemics/class_cells.sqlc reads `pg_type.typname` instead,
#   which is the name assets/ddl/schema.ddl wrote. `algebra/class_domain.sqlc` compares the two,
#   so a type renamed in the DDL fails here rather than silently classifying nothing.
-- the eight classifications, their codomains, the unit each counts in, and whether it is filed.
SELECT * FROM (VALUES
  ('arithmetic/all.sqlc',              'arithmetic_verdict', 'computation', true),
  ('layers/remainder.sqlc',            'fit',                'layer',       true),
  ('layers/remainder_scope.sqlc',      'remainder_standing', 'layer',       true),
  ('layers/exposure_scope.sqlc',       'exposure_standing',  'layer',       true),
  ('layers/quantities.sqlc',           'layer_quantity',     'quantity',    true),
  ('composition/part_references.sqlc', 'factor_state',       'part',        true),
  ('checks/fit_axes.sqlc',             'fit_axis',           'rule',        false),
  ('checks/fit_domain.sqlc',           'fit_standing',       'rule cell',   false)
) AS s(relation, codomain, subject, filed)
